{
  "title": "Predator particle feeding in Springwater basin",
  "engineSha256": "c3d496d7c9ef7ed3c33c24d7a8a5a303f47be16a86a9cceb27125cb7f58c30f2",
  "method": {
    "hungry": "The donor exports one soluble packet. After 34 seconds a swimming Predator with an empty gut and working inlet approaches it at nine bearings and two membrane gaps. Each replay starts from the same saved state. No further positioning occurs during the six seconds of observation. The current test also closes every inlet and checks for zero uptake and assimilation.",
    "funded": "A separate adult digests tissue before each saved encounter. During priming its ATP is replenished. After priming, a remote outlet makes a fresh equal-energy pulse for each genotype. Both trials report the free organic reserve, gut, ATP and delivered pulse energy at the start. A loss of the tracked pulse must coincide with a capture increment while this hunter is uniquely in range. A remote control verifies the pulse persists.",
    "mixed": "Bundled WebAssembly module under Node.js, counted chemistry, normal Springwater founders and 0.1 mutation. At every tick, integrate the sum of reported digestion rates in all live Predator descendants, in assimilated food energy. Also integrate the number of living Predators in seconds. These totals include both scavenging and tissue from predation. Each 30-second check balances carbon, mineral, oxygen and energy including work and heat losses.",
    "defaultScenario": "Ten-minute whole-basin run on the default launch seed. The lineage census includes mutated descendants."
  },
